    keyEmployers: ["Willis Towers Watson", "BT Adastral Park (nearby)", "AXA Insurance", "Ipswich Hospital", "Suffolk County Council"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "15-employee logistics and warehousing firm",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a39,192 of input VAT recoverable via a special method",
      body: "Take a 15 employee logistics and warehousing firm on Ipswich's Waterfront, running haulage to and from Felixstowe, warehousing, and some UK only distribution, with VAT returns filed on the standard partial exemption method throughout. Where the standard turnover based method does not reflect how input tax is actually used, a special method can be applied for, and the difference is often material. Suppose the two quarters under review carry \u00a376,600 of input tax in total and the standard method under recovers by 12 per cent on a mix of this kind. That is \u00a39,192 of input tax recoverable under a method that follows actual use. Payroll is a separate item: irregular shift patterns are worth structuring deliberately, because employer National Insurance runs at 15 per cent above the secondary threshold.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Ipswich?", answer: "We operate a remote-first model but hold regular in-person meetings in the Town Centre and at clients' premises across Ipswich, Felixstowe, and Bury St Edmunds. We can arrange a face to face session within a few days." },
      { question: "My tech business is near Adastral Park. Can you help with R&D tax credits?", answer: "Yes, and the first thing we look at near Adastral Park is not the finished product but the record of what did not work. A claim rests on an advance in science or technology and the technical uncertainty resolved, so the failed approaches carry as much evidential weight as the version that shipped. We prepare the technical narrative and the cost breakdown, and charge a fixed fee for that work, not a percentage of the claim." },
      { question: "How do you arrive at a fee for a limited company with one director in Ipswich?", answer: "On a short scoping call covering three things: how many transactions run through the company each month, whether you are VAT registered, and whether anyone is on the payroll. For a single-director company in Ipswich the answers are often low, yes and no, and the package is the accounts, the corporation tax return and your own return. A Port zone logistics business answers differently on all three." },
      { question: "I am a contractor working through Suffolk County Council. Do you handle IR35 reviews?", answer: "We do. We review your contract and working practices against HMRC's status tests, advise on your position, and can prepare your accounts and tax returns accordingly. We also offer a separate IR35 insurance signposting service." },
    ],
