    businessScene: "Sutton\u2019s business scene is unusual for a London suburb: it is a genuine hub for healthcare and life sciences, anchored by the Institute of Cancer Research and Royal Marsden Hospital. These institutions generate a steady flow of spin-outs, consultancy contracts and clinical supply businesses. Sutton Council is a major employer in its own right, and the legacy presence of Reed Elsevier (now RELX) has left a tech and data services talent pool that feeds into smaller digital agencies and software firms. The Town Centre and Wallington support a dense retail strip of independents and chains, while Cheam and Carshalton host professional practices and service businesses. Education is also significant: the borough\u2019s schools and colleges create demand for payroll and pension scheme compliance. The local economy is less volatile than central London\u2019s, but it faces pressure from rising business rates and the shift to hybrid working, which has reduced footfall in the Town Centre retail core.",
    geo: { latitude: 51.3618, longitude: -0.1944 },
    population: 200000,
    localSectors: ["Healthcare and life sciences", "Public sector", "Retail", "Tech", "Education"],
    sectorEmphasis: "Sutton\u2019s sector mix means our service emphasis is weighted toward corporation tax planning, R&D credits and payroll compliance. A healthcare consultancy working with the Institute of Cancer Research might come to us for R&D claim preparation on a clinical software project, while a retail business in the Town Centre often prioritises VAT compliance and capital allowances on shop fit-outs. Public sector contractors, including those supplying Sutton Council, need regular payroll and IR35 reviews. The tech and education sectors add further complexity: a Cheam-based software agency might need R&D credits on a new SaaS platform, and a Carshalton private school requires pension scheme administration and gift aid structuring. Because Sutton\u2019s businesses tend to be smaller and owner-managed, we also handle exit planning for those looking to sell to a larger group or pass the business to family. The common thread is that local clients value a firm that can move between sectors without losing precision on tax or compliance detail.",
    keyEmployers: ["Institute of Cancer Research", "Royal Marsden Hospital", "Sutton Council", "St Helier Hospital", "Reed Elsevier (legacy)"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "15-employee healthcare consultancy",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a318,278 R&D expenditure credit on clinical data software",
      body: "Consider a Sutton consultancy of 15 people supplying data analysis services to a large NHS hospital trust. Directors often assume this is standard contract research and therefore outside the R&D rules, when the qualifying activity sits in the algorithm development and in processing clinical trial data that arrives in inconsistent formats. The exercise is to test each project against the definition, cost the qualifying staff time, and cover both open accounting periods in one claim. Suppose the qualifying staff time across those periods comes to \u00a391,390. At the merged scheme rate of 20 per cent the expenditure credit is \u00a318,278 before the corporation tax charge on the credit itself. Businesses running a mix of employed analysts and freelance contractors also tend to find monthly payroll administration is simpler once the two groups are handled separately.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Sutton?", answer: "We are remote-first, but we meet clients in Sutton Town Centre or at their premises in Cheam, Wallington or Carshalton when needed. Most compliance work is handled online, which suits the professional services and healthcare businesses across the borough." },
      { question: "My Sutton-based tech business is developing new software. Can you help with R&D tax credits?", answer: "Yes, where there is a qualifying project. Software development counts only where it sought an advance in science or technology and the route to it was genuinely uncertain to a competent professional; building a familiar feature set or integrating existing services does not. For a Sutton business, whether a spin-out from the Institute of Cancer Research or an independent agency in Wallington, we assess that first and prepare the technical narrative and financial calculations where the project stands up. Claims run under the merged RDEC scheme for accounting periods beginning on or after 1 April 2024." },
      { question: "How do you handle pricing for a small retail business in the Town Centre?", answer: "Scoped to what a sole trader or micro-limited company actually needs across corporation tax, VAT and payroll, and payable monthly rather than in one sum. For a Town Centre retailer two things shape it: the till and the staff. A mixed-rate till means output VAT is computed through a retail scheme rather than line by line, and part-time staff bring auto-enrolment duties once earnings pass the trigger." },
      { question: "I supply services to Sutton Council. Do I need an IR35 review?", answer: "If you work through a limited company and your contract falls inside IR35, you could face unexpected tax bills. We review public sector contracts for Sutton-based contractors and advise on status determination statements and payroll adjustments." },
    ],
