    whyHere: "Bangor businesses operate in a distinctive mix of public sector, university spin outs, and tourism related enterprises. We work with limited company directors, contractors, and small business owners across City Centre, Upper Bangor, Maesgeirchen, and Hirael. Our team offers remote first support with in person meetings on request, giving local firms access to specialist corporation tax, VAT, and payroll advice without the overhead of a high street office.",
    businessScene: "Bangor's business scene is shaped by its role as a regional hub for education and healthcare. Bangor University employs over 2,500 people and drives demand for research, commercialisation, and spin out support. Ysbyty Gwynedd is the main acute hospital for Gwynedd and Anglesey, anchoring a healthcare supply chain that includes clinics, care homes, and medical suppliers. The public sector is a major employer, with Gwynedd Council and the Snowdonia National Park Authority based locally. Tourism is significant, with the city acting as a gateway to Snowdonia and the Menai Strait, supporting a network of hotels, outdoor activity providers, and hospitality businesses. Marine and environmental research is concentrated at the university's School of Ocean Sciences and the RSPB's regional office in nearby Conwy. The local economy is relatively small scale, with many businesses operating as sole traders, partnerships, or limited companies serving a predominantly local and visitor based market.",
    geo: { latitude: 53.228, longitude: -4.129 },
    population: 18000,
    localSectors: ["Higher education", "Healthcare", "Tourism", "Marine and environmental research", "Public sector"],
    sectorEmphasis: "The local economic mix in Bangor means our service emphasis is tailored to each sector. A university spin out in marine biotechnology might come to us for R&D tax credit preparation, where we identify qualifying costs on laboratory equipment and consumables. A tourism business operating a hotel or outdoor activity centre in the Snowdonia National Park Authority area often needs help with VAT partial exemption and capital allowances on fixtures and fittings. Healthcare contractors working at Ysbyty Gwynedd, such as locum doctors or physiotherapists, typically require payroll and IR35 compliance support. Public sector subcontractors and consultants engaged by Gwynedd Council or the university often need assistance with corporation tax and dividend planning. We also see a steady flow of limited company directors in the retail and hospitality sectors across City Centre and Upper Bangor, where VAT registration thresholds and property related tax issues are common. Local businesses get the confidence that their affairs are handled to professional standards, whether they are a 10 employee marketing agency or a sole trader running a B&B in Hirael.",
    keyEmployers: ["Bangor University", "Ysbyty Gwynedd (hospital)", "Royal Society for the Protection of Birds (regional)", "Snowdonia National Park Authority", "Gwynedd Council"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "8 employee marine research consultancy",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a312,300 net R&D credit on \u00a382,000 of spend",
      body: "Take a Bangor marine research consultancy with offices in Upper Bangor and field operations on the Menai Strait, three full time researchers and five contractors, working on sensor development part funded by a university and a conservation body. Grant funding does not automatically kill a claim, but it changes which scheme applies, so the funding terms have to be read alongside the project records. Suppose software development, prototype testing and environmental monitoring give \u00a382,000 of qualifying expenditure over two years. Under the merged expenditure credit scheme at 20 per cent that is a \u00a316,400 gross credit, worth \u00a312,300 net after corporation tax at the 25 per cent main rate.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Bangor?", answer: "We are a remote first firm with no physical office in Bangor. We meet clients at their premises or at a neutral location in City Centre or Upper Bangor when needed. Video calls are the default for routine tax and accounts work, which matters across a catchment where travel eats the day." },
      { question: "I run a tourism business near Snowdonia. Can you help with VAT on accommodation?", answer: "Yes. Tourism businesses in the Bangor area, including hotels and self catering properties, often need advice on VAT partial exemption and the tour operators margin scheme. We can review your income streams and advise on the most efficient VAT treatment." },
      { question: "How is the fee agreed when I switch from my current accountant?", answer: "Switching is a scoping exercise before it is anything else. We ask for the last filed accounts, the tax computations and the VAT history, because those show what is genuinely outstanding: an unclaimed R&D position on a spin-out's sensor development, or a marine consultancy sitting on lab equipment that was never pooled. Professional clearance and the transfer of records we deal with ourselves." },
      { question: "I am a contractor working at Ysbyty Gwynedd. Do you handle IR35 assessments?", answer: "Yes. We regularly advise healthcare contractors and locums on IR35 status and provide contract reviews. If you operate through a limited company, we can help structure your engagements to stay outside IR35 where appropriate, and prepare your annual accounts and corporation tax return." },
    ],
