    businessScene: "Stevenage's economy is defined by its concentration of aerospace and defence employers. MBDA Missile Systems and Airbus Defence and Space occupy large sites near the Town Centre, driving demand for specialist subcontractors and engineering consultancies. The pharmaceutical sector is anchored by GSK Stevenage and the Stevenage Bioscience Catalyst, a science park that incubates early-stage biotech and medtech ventures. Lister Hospital adds a significant public sector and healthcare employment base. Tech firms, including software developers and data analytics outfits, have grown around the Old Town and Chells areas. The mix of large corporate tenants and smaller supply-chain businesses creates a distinctive advisory need: one part compliance, one part growth planning. Nearby towns Hertford, Hitchin, Letchworth and Welwyn Garden City extend the same economic profile, and we serve those clients from the same team.",
    geo: { latitude: 51.902, longitude: -0.2024 },
    population: 88000,
    localSectors: ["Aerospace and defence", "Pharmaceutical", "Tech", "Healthcare", "Public sector"],
    sectorEmphasis: "The local sector mix shapes how we organise our work. A defence subcontractor in Pin Green might need R&D credits on a new guidance-system prototype, while a biotech tenant at Stevenage Bioscience Catalyst often requires capital allowances on lab fit-outs and patent-box planning. GSK Stevenage's supply chain generates recurring VAT and payroll work for specialist logistics and cleanroom maintenance firms. Lister Hospital's GP partnerships and private healthcare providers come to us for practice-specific tax structuring and pension optimisation. Tech companies in the Old Town frequently ask about EMI share schemes and exit planning. We handle cross-border issues for aerospace firms with international contracts, and we prepare R&D claim reports that stand up to HMRC review. The common thread across Stevenage's sectors is a need for accountants who understand capital-intensive, innovation-driven businesses rather than generic bookkeeping.",
    keyEmployers: ["MBDA Missile Systems", "Airbus Defence and Space", "GSK Stevenage", "Lister Hospital", "Stevenage Bioscience Catalyst"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "8-employee defence engineering consultancy",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a322,400 expenditure credit on radar simulation software",
      body: "Take a Chells defence consultancy with eight engineers that has built a radar simulation tool for a subcontract on a next-generation missile programme, with three years of software development and no R&D claim made. Suppose the records support \u00a3112,000 of eligible staff costs and consumables across the two accounting periods still in time. Under the RDEC scheme at 20 per cent, that is an expenditure credit of \u00a322,400, which is itself taxable, so the net cash is lower than the headline. Claims are capped by reference to PAYE and NIC, so payroll records matter as much as the technical narrative. Exit planning for founding directors is a separate exercise that a settled tax position makes easier.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Stevenage?", answer: "We are remote-first but meet Stevenage clients in person at their premises or at a co-working space in the Town Centre. We cover SG1 and SG2 postcodes and regularly see clients in Chells, Old Town and Pin Green." },
      { question: "What services do Stevenage biotech and pharma companies typically need?", answer: "Pharma and biotech clients at Stevenage Bioscience Catalyst often require R&D tax credit preparation, capital allowances on lab equipment, and patent box planning. We also handle payroll for research teams and VAT on cross-border clinical trial supplies." },
      { question: "How do you handle pricing for a small defence subcontractor?", answer: "Compliance is quoted on transaction volume and complexity: corporation tax, VAT and payroll for a subcontractor with a small workforce. R&D claims are scoped as separate project work, which matters here because a defence subcontractor's technical narrative often has to be written around what can and cannot be described. How that is handled is agreed before the work starts rather than at submission." },
      { question: "Can you take over from a previous accountant mid-year?", answer: "Yes. We handle the handover directly with your existing accountant, review your last two years of filings for errors or missed reliefs, and take over the agent authorisations at HMRC and Companies House. We plan the changeover around your next filing date rather than to a fixed schedule." },
    ],
