    businessScene: "Penzance's economy is anchored by tourism, fishing and maritime industries, alongside a significant public sector presence. The Isles of Scilly Steamship Company operates the passenger and freight link to the islands, supporting both tourism and local supply chains. Penlee Lifeboat (RNLI) and West Cornwall Hospital provide substantial employment, while Cornwall Council maintains a regional office presence. The Town Centre and Newlyn harbour area host a mix of retail, hospitality and fishing related businesses. Stein's Fish & Chips, a legacy employer, reflects the enduring importance of the seafood sector. Seasonal tourism drives demand for accommodation, food service and leisure businesses, while healthcare and public sector roles provide year round stability. The local economy is distinctive for its reliance on maritime infrastructure and the visitor economy, with many small businesses operating across both sectors.",
    geo: { latitude: 50.1188, longitude: -5.5371 },
    population: 21000,
    localSectors: ["Tourism", "Fishing and maritime", "Public sector", "Retail", "Healthcare"],
    sectorEmphasis: "The local sector mix in Penzance shapes our service emphasis in several ways. A tourism business in the Town Centre, such as a hotel or self catering operator, often needs help with VAT partial exemption calculations and seasonal payroll planning. We structure their returns to match the summer peak and winter trough, avoiding unnecessary quarterly payments on account. A Newlyn based fishing or seafood processing business, like one supplying Stein's Fish & Chips, typically requires capital allowances on vessels and processing equipment, plus careful VAT treatment of zero rated food sales. We also see R&D claims from local engineering and marine technology firms developing new fishing gear or processing methods. Public sector contractors, including those working with Cornwall Council or West Cornwall Hospital, come to us for IR35 reviews and limited company tax planning. Healthcare professionals, such as GPs and physiotherapists running their own practices, need corporation tax, payroll and pension advice. Across all sectors, clients get the confidence that their affairs are handled correctly, particularly when HMRC queries arise on complex maritime or tourism related returns.",
    keyEmployers: ["Isles of Scilly Steamship Company", "Penlee Lifeboat (RNLI)", "West Cornwall Hospital", "Cornwall Council (regional)", "Stein's Fish & Chips (legacy)"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "8 employee seafood processor",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a344,225 of capital allowances on a processing line",
      body: "Consider a Newlyn seafood processor with eight staff that buys a second hand fishing vessel and installs an automated filleting line, treating the lot as a single standard asset. Separating the purchase invoices identifies \u00a3176,900 of qualifying expenditure across the hull modifications and the filleting plant. At the 25% main corporation tax rate that expenditure is worth \u00a344,225 off the year\u2019s liability. Fish sales raise a separate question: zero rating applies to most unprocessed and prepared food sold for consumption off the premises, but not to everything a processor sells, and the liability decision belongs on the invoice line rather than on the customer account.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Penzance?", answer: "We are remote first, so we do not maintain a permanent office in Penzance. We meet clients on request at a location convenient to them, such as a caf\u00e9 in the Town Centre or a meeting room in Newlyn. Video calls are the default, which saves a long drive for anyone west of Hayle." },
      { question: "My tourism business has seasonal staff. How do you handle payroll?", answer: "We set up payroll on a variable basis, processing payments only when staff are employed. We handle RTI submissions, pension auto enrolment for eligible staff, and P45s at the end of each season. This avoids the administrative burden of maintaining a payroll record for zero hours periods." },
      { question: "How is the fee worked out for a limited company tax return in Penzance?", answer: "On what the company holds. A single-director company with modest turnover is quickly scoped. A Newlyn seafood business is not: a vessel and a processing line raise capital allowance questions, and zero-rated food output usually means VAT registration produces a repayment rather than a bill, which changes the decision about registering at all. Several director shareholders add dividend documentation and more than one personal return." },
      { question: "Can you help with VAT for a Newlyn fishing business?", answer: "Yes. Fishing businesses often have complex VAT positions due to zero rated fish sales, partially exempt supplies and capital goods on vessels. We review your turnover, input tax and any exempt supplies to ensure you are not overpaying or under reclaiming. We also handle VAT registration and quarterly returns." },
    ],
