    intro: "Paignton\u2019s economy is anchored by tourism, retail, and a growing healthcare sector, with Torbay Hospital and Paignton Zoo as major local employers.",
    whyHere: "Paignton businesses range from seasonal hospitality operators on the seafront to established manufacturers in Marldon. We serve them from a remote-first base, meeting clients in the Town Centre or Preston as needed. The mix of tourism, retail, and public sector work demands a generalist approach: corporation tax for a B&B, VAT for a gift shop, payroll for a care home. Local directors get the confidence that the numbers are right, whether they are filing quarterly or planning an exit.",
    businessScene: "Paignton\u2019s business scene is shaped by its coastal location and a population of around 50,000. Tourism and hospitality dominate the Town Centre and Goodrington, with attractions such as Quaywest Waterpark and Paignton Zoo drawing visitors seasonally. Retail is concentrated along the high street, while healthcare is a major employer thanks to Torbay Hospital in neighbouring Torquay. Manufacturing remains present in pockets like Marldon, and Torbay Council provides a steady public sector anchor. The local economy is distinctive for its reliance on seasonal spending, which creates cashflow volatility for many small businesses. That volatility drives demand for reliable bookkeeping, VAT planning, and payroll management across the year.",
    geo: { latitude: 50.435, longitude: -3.5645 },
    population: 50000,
    localSectors: ["Tourism and hospitality", "Retail", "Healthcare", "Manufacturing", "Public sector"],
    sectorEmphasis: "The local sector mix shapes how we prioritise services for Paignton clients. A hotel or B&B in Goodrington often needs help with VAT partial exemption on mixed supplies (accommodation versus food and drink). A retail shop in the Town Centre might come to us for payroll set up as they take on seasonal staff. Healthcare contractors linked to Torbay Hospital frequently require IR35 reviews and limited company accounting. Manufacturers in Marldon, such as those in precision engineering, are candidates for R&D tax credits on process improvements. Public sector subcontractors working with Torbay Council often need assistance with CIS returns and year end filings. Because Paignton has no single dominant industry, we keep a broad service line: corporation tax, VAT, payroll, R&D credits, and exit planning are all in regular demand. That breadth is the point. A local business gets one accountant who understands the full picture, not a specialist who only sees one tax type.",
    keyEmployers: ["Paignton Zoo", "Torbay Hospital (Torquay nearby)", "Devonshire Park Theatre (Eastbourne legacy)", "Torbay Council", "Quaywest Waterpark"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "15 employee tourism operator",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a331,400 of overpaid VAT on mixed supplies",
      body: "Consider a Paignton tourism operator running a caf\u00e9 and self catering apartments in Goodrington, with one till applying the standard rate to everything. Cold takeaway food is zero rated, sit in food is standard rated, and holiday accommodation is standard rated, so a single rate across the whole till guarantees an error in one direction. Splitting the booking and till systems into accommodation, sit in and takeaway streams, suppose the restated records show \u00a3188,400 of cold takeaway sales rung through at the standard rate across the four year correction window. At the VAT fraction of one sixth that is \u00a331,400 of overpaid output tax. The four year cap is the binding constraint: anything overpaid before it is simply lost, which is why the split is worth doing before the next return rather than after.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Paignton?", answer: "We are a remote first firm, but we meet clients in the Town Centre or Preston by arrangement. Most of the Paignton work is done online through a secure portal, which suits hospitality and retail owners whose season leaves little room for daytime meetings." },
      { question: "My B&B has both accommodation and food sales. How is VAT handled?", answer: "Holiday and hotel accommodation is standard rated at 20 per cent, the same as your food and drink. The temporary 5 per cent rate ended on 30 September 2021, and accommodation of this kind is outside the VAT exemption for land, so it is not a partial exemption question either: partial exemption only arises where you make exempt supplies. What does move the numbers is the food side, where cold takeaway is zero rated while sit-in and hot takeaway are standard rated. We help you separate the income streams so the returns are right and the input tax you are entitled to is recovered in full." },
      { question: "I am a contractor working at Torbay Hospital. Do you handle IR35 reviews?", answer: "Yes. An NHS trust is not a small client, so Torbay Hospital makes the status determination and must issue it with reasons; your own reading of the contract does not override it, although there is a disagreement process. We review the contract against how the work is actually done, and where you are outside the rules we handle the year-end accounts and the dividend documentation that goes with extracting profit properly." },
      { question: "How does pricing work for a small retail business in the Town Centre in Paignton?", answer: "On transaction volume, which for a Town Centre retailer means till takings rather than sales invoices. The scope covers bookkeeping and the annual accounts. Two things commonly get added: seasonal payroll when summer staff arrive, and a VAT scheme review, since a shop selling a mix of zero-rated and standard-rated goods computes output VAT through a retail scheme rather than item by item." },
    ],
