    intro: "Uxbridge sits at the intersection of the M40, M25 and the A40, making it a logistics and pharma hub with a strong tech and higher education presence.",
    whyHere: "Uxbridge\u2019s mix of corporate HQs (Coca-Cola European Partners, Cadent Gas), Brunel University and a dense retail core creates a varied client base. We meet directors at their Cowley or Town Centre offices, or work remotely across UB8, UB9 and UB10. Local business owners get the confidence that the tax and compliance work is done to a professional standard, whether they run a logistics firm or a tech startup.",
    businessScene: "Uxbridge is a genuine business crossroads. Coca-Cola European Partners and Cadent Gas anchor the corporate end, while GE Healthcare maintains a regional presence in the Hillingdon area. Brunel University drives a steady flow of spin-outs and R&D intensive projects in engineering and digital health. The logistics and pharma sectors cluster around the M40 corridor, with warehousing and distribution centres in Yiewsley and Cowley. Hillingdon Council is a major employer in its own right, and the Town Centre supports a broad retail and hospitality sector. The local economy is distinctive for its blend of multinational HQs, public sector institutions and independent contractors who service them all. This diversity means we see everything from VAT planning for a Yiewsley haulier to share scheme advice for a Brunel-linked tech firm.",
    geo: { latitude: 51.5455, longitude: -0.4779 },
    population: 70000,
    localSectors: ["Logistics and pharma", "Tech", "Higher education", "Public sector", "Retail"],
    sectorEmphasis: "Uxbridge\u2019s economic mix shapes our service emphasis in clear ways. A logistics or pharma business in Cowley or Yiewsley typically needs corporation tax planning on capital allowances for warehouse fit-outs and vehicle fleets, plus VAT work on cross-border supply chains. We handle those regularly. The tech and university-linked sector around Brunel University brings a different set of needs: R&D credit preparation for software and engineering projects, EMI scheme setup for early stage teams, and exit planning for founders who have grown a business from a university lab. A Brunel spin-out in digital health preparing an R&D claim on a new diagnostics platform has to split qualifying costs across software development and clinical trials, and the trial spend is where eligibility is most often argued. Meanwhile, Hillingdon Council and other public sector bodies create demand for payroll and pension compliance among contractors and interim managers. Retail and hospitality businesses in the Town Centre rely on us for VAT returns, payroll and year end accounts. The common thread is that we adapt the technical work to the local sector reality, not the other way around.",
    keyEmployers: ["Coca-Cola European Partners HQ", "Cadent Gas HQ", "Brunel University", "Hillingdon Council", "GE Healthcare (regional)"],
    localCaseStudy: {
      business_type: "15 employee logistics and pharma distributor",
      headline: "Worked example: \u00a310,500 corporation tax saving on a Cowley cold store",
      body: "Take a Yiewsley distributor of temperature controlled pharma products spending \u00a395,000 on a new cold storage facility in Cowley. Capitalising the whole cost as general plant and machinery misses the split: building services and insulation are integral features falling into the special rate pool. Where \u00a342,000 of the spend is qualifying expenditure and the Annual Investment Allowance covers it in full, relief at the 25 per cent main rate of corporation tax is worth \u00a310,500. A parallel question on intra EU movements after Brexit is worth modelling too, since a supply chain that puts the company in the right place in the chain can unblock input tax that was previously irrecoverable.",
    },
    localFaqs: [
      { question: "Do you have an office in Uxbridge?", answer: "We are remote first and keep no Uxbridge office. We meet clients at their premises in Cowley, Yiewsley or the Town Centre, or at a coffee shop near Brunel University. Video calls carry most routine matters, and documents move through a secure portal." },
      { question: "My business is a Brunel University spin out. Do you handle R&D tax credits for early stage tech companies?", answer: "Yes. We prepare R&D claims for Brunel-linked startups and spin-outs across engineering, digital health and software, and we also advise on EMI share schemes and investor-ready financials. For a spin-out two issues usually decide the claim: documenting the technical uncertainty while the work is being done rather than reconstructing it a year later, and choosing between the merged RDEC scheme and Enhanced R&D Intensive Support, which is open to a loss-making company whose R&D is at least 30 per cent of total expenditure." },
      { question: "How do you charge for a limited company based in Uxbridge?", answer: "On transaction volume and what has to be filed. For a one-director company with basic VAT and payroll that is settled quickly. A Cowley logistics or pharma distributor is not: a warehouse fit-out has to be split between plant, integral features in the special rate pool at 6%, and the structure itself, and that split is worth getting right once because it governs the relief for years afterwards." },
      { question: "I run a retail business in Uxbridge Town Centre. Do you handle VAT and payroll for small shops?", answer: "We do. We prepare VAT returns, process payroll for part time staff and file year end accounts for retailers across the Town Centre and Hillingdon. We also advise on flat rate VAT schemes if your turnover is below the threshold." },
    ],
